```python
new = (
    G.run(repo, *args, input=msg.out + "\n")
    if all(r.ok for r in (added, tree, head, msg))
    else added
)
if new.ok and G.run(repo, "update-ref", "HEAD", new.out.strip(), head.out.strip()).ok:
```

When the `all(...)` guard is false, `new` is bound to the result of `git add -A` (`added`) rather than to a `commit-tree` result. A successful `git add` prints nothing, so `new.out.strip()` is `""`, while `new.ok` is still true — the `if new.ok` guard then lets the code run `git update-ref HEAD "" <head-sha>`. An empty `<new-oid>` is update-ref's deletion form, so the ref that `HEAD` dereferences (the branch that was just merged, e.g. `refs/heads/main`) can be deleted instead of advanced; at best the call fails and the failure is reported as `f"pins regenerated, not committed ({new.err})"` using `git add`'s (empty) stderr, masking the real plumbing error. Trigger: any merge where the post-merge hook runs, `git add -A -- <pin files>` succeeds, but one of `git write-tree` / `git rev-parse HEAD` / `git log -1 HEAD` returns non-zero (index write failure, damaged/ambiguous HEAD), which is exactly what the `else` branch exists for. The fallback must not be a `git add` result.
